<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
    <artifactId>eyeandsickle-parent</artifactId>
    <version>${revision}</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>eyeandsickle-server</artifactId>
  <name>The Eye and Sickle :: Home Server</name>
  <description>
    The self-hostable home server (docs/architecture/01 §2, 03).

    Owns and validates ALL game state for its players in PostgreSQL. This module
    is authoritative: anything a cheating client could lie about — item
    ownership, ethecoin balances, compute allocation, duel outcomes — is decided
    here (Invariants I14 / I15).

    This module is, and must stay, a reactor LEAF. spring-boot-maven-plugin's
    `repackage` goal rewrites the main artifact into a fat jar whose classes sit
    under BOOT-INF/classes, which is not resolvable as a normal Maven
    dependency. If something ever needs to depend on server code, split a plain
    `server-core` jar out below this module rather than depending on the
    repackaged artifact — the enforcer rule below makes that failure loud.
  </description>

  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <!--
        Imported HERE and not in the root pom on purpose. An import-scope BOM in
        the parent is inherited by every module, so a Spring Boot upgrade would
        silently retarget the client's Jackson/SLF4J/JUnit versions too. Keeping
        it local means Boot manages exactly the module that asked for it.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>
        <version>${spring-boot.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>

      <!--
        ⚠ H2 IS PINNED BELOW WHAT SPRING BOOT MANAGES, AND THIS IS NOT A STYLE
        CHOICE. Boot 4.1's BOM brings H2 2.4.240, which has a regression that
        makes every CHECK constraint in this schema permanently dead:

          h2database/h2database#4291 (dup #4292, #4320, #4342)

        2.4.240 evaluates a CHECK against the session that PARSED it, not the
        session doing the write — `ConditionInConstantSet` keeps the parsing
        SessionLocal as its TreeSet comparator. Flyway applies the migrations on
        its own connection and closes it, so from that moment on every insert
        into `players`, `items`, `ledger_transactions`, `breach_resolutions` and
        `provenance_records` fails with

          [23514] Check constraint invalid: "ck_players_faction: "

        ⚠ The failure mode is worse than it looks. 23514 is "check constraint
        INVALID", not 23513 "violated" — the constraint did not reject the row,
        it could not be evaluated — and H2 drops the cause, so the message names
        a constraint the row satisfies perfectly. It is not confined to `IN`
        lists either: `a = 'x' OR a = 'y'` and `n IN (1,2,3)` fail identically.
        Measured on H2 2.4.240 and 2.3.232, both spellings, memory and file.

        ⚠ A file database RE-PARSES its constraints when the database is next
        opened, so the bug looks intermittent: broken from the migration until
        a restart, fine afterwards. With a pool it is worse still — Flyway's
        connection is returned rather than closed, so the checks work until
        Hikari evicts that connection on idleTimeout and then stop.

        2.4.240 is the newest release and the fix is unreleased, so pinning back
        is the whole remedy. A locally-declared dependencyManagement entry beats
        an imported BOM, which is why this works from here.

        REMOVE THIS PIN when an H2 > 2.4.240 ships the fix — and re-run
        SchemaIT, which is what catches it. The version itself lives in the
        root pom as ${h2.version}, shared with the solo module.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <version>${h2.version}</version>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-protocol</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- REST for CRUD; WebSocket for live engagements (breaches, raid alerts,
         join notifications) — docs/architecture/01 §5 [PROPOSAL]. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-websocket</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      All game state lives in this server's Postgres (Invariant I14).

      RESOLVED (open question A-4, docs/design/15): Spring's JdbcClient over
      hand-written SQL, mapping to Java records. NOT JPA/Hibernate, NOT jOOQ.

      Three reasons, in order of weight:
        1. The signed crypto payloads and item attrs are document-shaped jsonb
           (docs/architecture/06 §4). JPA is awkward with jsonb; JdbcClient just
           passes it through.
        2. The manual-audit gameplay (docs/design/04 §3.1) runs against the
           compute ledger, and Invariant I6 makes a *discrepancy* the signal a
           player is hunting for. Queries that carry that meaning should be
           legible SQL, not a derived ORM projection.
        3. jOOQ was the close runner-up and is arguably the better fit for the
           relational core — but its codegen wants a live database at build time,
           which would break "mvn verify must never require Docker".

      Flyway owns the schema; nothing here alters it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jdbc</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      H2 — the database, embedded. Replaced PostgreSQL entirely on 2026-08-02.

      ⚠ COMPILE scope, not runtime, and that is a real change in kind. A JDBC
      driver is a runtime detail; H2 is not, because the schema's ENFORCEMENT is
      now implemented against its API. `persistence/AppendOnlyTrigger` and
      `MonotonicSequenceTrigger` implement org.h2.api.Trigger, and the DID and
      JSON CHECK constraints are H2 ALIASes onto our own static methods.

      So the server's code is coupled to H2, not merely its configuration. That
      is the price of keeping the append-only and anti-rollback guarantees INSIDE
      the engine — where they fire whatever code path issued the write — rather
      than moving them up into the service layer where a new caller can miss them.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
      <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      MUST be the STARTER, not a bare org.flywaydb:flyway-core.

      Spring Boot 4 split spring-boot-autoconfigure into per-technology modules,
      so FlywayAutoConfiguration now lives in org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-flyway
      and nothing else drags it in. With only flyway-core on the classpath the
      Flyway classes load fine, `mvn verify` is green, and every spring.flyway.*
      key in application.yml binds to nothing — migrations simply never run. That
      failure is invisible until a self-hoster's database is silently empty.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Flyway 10+ moved per-database support into its own artifact. H2 support
         lives in flyway-core itself, so this is the only one needed. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- AT Protocol OAuth, AUTHENTICATION ONLY (docs/architecture/02).
         The player's DID is the portable ID; no game state is ever written to
         a player's PDS. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The rules engine.

      ⚠ This is the refactor that removed a duplicate-rules problem rather than creating one.
      CLAUDE.md used to warn that `solo` was "a SECOND IMPLEMENTATION of a subset of the rules" and
      that re-tuning design/03 meant re-reading solo/Balance.java. That was true while the plan was
      for the server to grow its own engine. It no longer is: `solo` IS the engine, and where its
      state lives is a detail behind the SaveStore interface — a file for single player, this
      server's Postgres for LAN and federated.

      ⚠ Invariant I14 is satisfied by that, not weakened. I14 says game state lives in the server's
      database and never in player-controlled infrastructure; a server-side SaveStore keeps it
      exactly there, and the engine that decides anything runs here against server-held state.

      solo carries no Spring, no driver, no HTTP stack and no thread of its own — its own enforcer
      rule holds that line — so depending on it adds nothing to this module's tree but the rules.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-engine</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      BouncyCastle, for ONE reason: secp256k1.

      Most did:plc accounts sign with secp256k1, and SunEC cannot verify it (measured on OpenJDK 26 —
      the curve name resolves, a key constructs, initVerify SUCCEEDS, and only verify() refuses). So
      without this the server cannot check the service-auth JWT of the majority of real players.
      docs/architecture/10-oauth-and-did-resolution.md section 5.1 has the measured table.

      SERVER ONLY, deliberately. The client needs P-256 for DPoP and Ed25519 for provenance, both of
      which the platform has everywhere — so the client's dependency austerity is untouched and this
      is not carried into the five platform jars or the jpackage image.

      Registering it is NOT sufficient by itself: SunEC still answers getInstance() first and fails
      later, so the provider must be named explicitly. protocol identity MultibaseKey probes for a
      provider that actually works and remembers which one.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.bouncycastle</groupId>
      <artifactId>bcprov-jdk18on</artifactId>
      <version>${bouncycastle.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      OpenAPI 3.1 generation for the REST surface (docs/architecture/14).

      ⚠ The SPEC is the deliverable; Swagger UI comes with the starter and is a
      convenience. Both are OFF BY DEFAULT — see application.yml. A self-hosted
      home server is closed by default (allowlist, docs/architecture/03 §1), and
      an interactive API console answering to whoever can reach the port is not
      the kind of thing to switch on for somebody else's box without asking.

      ⚠ The `-ui` starter rather than the bare `-api` one, because the two
      properties that disable them are independent: `springdoc.api-docs.enabled`
      and `springdoc.swagger-ui.enabled`. Taking the smaller artifact would mean
      an operator who wants the console has to change a dependency rather than a
      setting, which is how a documented API ends up undocumented in practice.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
      <version>${springdoc.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <!--
            Give the executable fat jar a classifier, so the PLAIN jar stays the module's default
            artifact. Without this, repackage rewrites the one-and-only jar into the fat-jar layout
            (classes under BOOT-INF/), and then a @SpringBootTest integration test run by failsafe —
            which happens AFTER the package phase — cannot find the application class on its
            classpath. This is the concrete form of the CLAUDE.md warning that repackage rewrites the
            jar; the classifier keeps a normal jar around alongside the runnable one.
            Run the server with: java -jar eyeandsickle-server-<version>-boot.jar
          -->
          <classifier>boot</classifier>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <goals><goal>repackage</goal></goals>
          </execution>
          <!--
            Writes META-INF/build-info.properties, which Boot turns into a
            BuildProperties bean. The OpenAPI document reads its version from
            there rather than carrying a literal: a hard-coded version string is
            correct on the day it is written and wrong from the next release,
            and a reader has no way to tell a stale one from a current one.
            ⚠ The bean is ABSENT when the goal has not run (an IDE launch, a
            plain `mvn test`), so OpenApiConfiguration takes it as an
            ObjectProvider and falls back rather than failing to start.
          -->
          <execution>
            <id>build-info</id>
            <goals><goal>build-info</goal></goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>server-has-no-ui</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <message>
                    The home server must not depend on JavaFX or on the client
                    module. Rendering belongs to the client; authority belongs
                    to the server, and the boundary is one-directional.
                  </message>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>org.openjfx:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>io.github.mkpaz:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle:eyeandsickle-client</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
